<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tự Đức</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1829">1829</a>–<a href="/years/1883">1883</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tu Duc</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tự Đức was emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> under the Nguyen dynasty from 1847 to 1883, its longest-reigning ruler. A scholarly Confucian, he persecuted Catholics and resisted reform. Under French attack he ceded three eastern provinces of Cochinchina by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saigon-1862">Treaty of Saigon</a> in 1862, and lost the western three in 1867. His death was followed by the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tu-duc.jpg" alt="Tự Đức" width="320" height="371" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Docteur Rieux at Vietnamese Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vua_Tu_Duc.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tự Đức, whose personal name was Nguyễn Phúc Hồng Nhậm, was born on 22 September 1829, a son of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thieu-tri">Thiệu Trị</a>. He succeeded his father in 1847 as the fourth emperor of the Nguyễn dynasty. Thiệu Trị had passed over his more moderate eldest son Hồng Bảo in favour of Tự Đức, who was known for his staunch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> and his opposition to foreigners and innovation. Dissatisfaction with Nguyễn rule was already widespread because of the repressive policies of the previous emperor, and Hồng Bảo offered a legitimate royal figure around whom opponents could gather.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Hồng Bảo plotted rebellion with Confucians, supporters of the Lê dynasty, corrupt mandarins, Catholic missionaries and converts, and peasants unhappy with taxation. He was arrested in 1854 and killed himself in prison. In 1866 his son Đinh Đạo rebelled, and after the revolt was crushed Tự Đức ordered Đinh Đạo, his brothers and his mother put to death. A cholera epidemic in 1849 killed about 600,000 according to the royal archives, and droughts from 1864 and typhoons in Tonkin in 1880 and 1881 added to the strain.</p><p>Tự Đức kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> closed to the outside world and refused efforts at modernisation. He oppressed foreigners and above all Christians, calling their religion a perverse doctrine. An order of 1848 commanded Catholic converts to renounce their faith or be branded on the face. The Christian mandarin Nguyễn Trường Tộ urged reform forty-three times without success, and a plan for a school of sciences and crafts at Huế was dropped. In the north, Chinese bands such as the White Flags and Yellow Flags invaded Tonkin, and the court hired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-flag-army">Black Flag Army</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yongfu">Liu Yongfu</a> against them.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> sent an expeditionary force that attacked up from the south in 1858 to 1862, using the execution of a Spanish bishop as justification. Faced with rebellions as the French advanced, Tự Đức preferred a deal with France to the risk of being deposed. He signed away Cochinchina as a colony, which caused uproar, and officials such as Trương Định fought on. In 1876 and 1880 he sent missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> to affirm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>'s tributary status to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tự Đức died on 19 July 1883, the longest-reigning Nguyễn emperor and the last Vietnamese monarch to rule independently. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">Smallpox</a> had left him unable to father children, and his adopted son Dục Đức, who succeeded him, was deposed by court officials after three days. According to legend, he cursed the French with his dying breath.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tự Đức” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Catholicism, spread by missionaries since the seventeenth century, had a few hundred thousand adherents; Gia Long tolerated it out of gratitude to Pigneau, but Minh Mang issued edicts against it from 1833, and missionaries and Vietnamese Catholics were executed under him and under <b>Tu Duc</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Saigon in 1862 <b>Emperor Tu Duc</b> of the Nguyen dynasty ceded three eastern provinces, and in 1867 Admiral de La Grandiere seized the three western ones, completing the colony of Cochinchina.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-cochinchina">French Cochinchina</a></li><li><blockquote>After the French captured Hanoi and the death of <b>Emperor Tu Duc</b>, the Harmand Treaty of 1883 and the Patenotre Treaty of 1884 forced the Nguyen dynasty to accept French protection.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-of-annam">French protectorate of Annam</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Huế, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in Huế, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=16.46,107.58&amp;year=1864" aria-label="Huế on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7480 1552 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tự Đức"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7930 1822h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7930 1822h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=16.46,107.58&amp;year=1864">What was here in 1864? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
